Subject: [PATCH] net: dedicated NetLog events for the SOCKS5 authentication phase

Patch 0001 added RFC 1929 authentication and reused the existing SOCKS event
types for it, because dedicated ones needed a new entry in
net_log_event_type_list.h. Three of those reuses are wrong in a way that costs
a real diagnosis:

  A rejected credential logged SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH, whose documented meaning
  in that file is "the SOCKS proxy server asked for an authentication method
  that we don't support". So a wrong password and a proxy offering only GSSAPI
  produced the same event, and they need opposite fixes.

  A bad RFC 1929 subnegotiation version logged SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_VERSION, which
  reads as a bad SOCKS version. The subnegotiation carries its own version
  octet, fixed at 0x01, which has nothing to do with the 0x05 in the greeting.

  A proxy closing the connection mid-authentication logged
  SOCKS_UNEXPECTEDLY_CLOSED_DURING_GREETING, naming a phase that had already
  finished successfully.

There was also no phase event at all for the authentication exchange, so a
net-export dump went straight from SOCKS5_GREET_READ to
SOCKS5_HANDSHAKE_WRITE whether or not credentials had been sent, and the
timing of a slow authenticating proxy was attributed to the CONNECT request.

This adds five event types next to the existing SOCKS5ClientSocket block and
uses them: SOCKS5_AUTH_WRITE and SOCKS5_AUTH_READ as begin/end phase events
alongside the greet and handshake pairs, and SOCKS5_AUTH_REJECTED,
SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTED_VERSION and SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTEDLY_CLOSED in place of
the three misreported failures. The greeting's own method-selection failure
keeps SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH, where that event's documented meaning is exactly
right.

NetLog is a debugging facility. It is not page-visible, no byte on the wire
changes, and the unauthenticated greeting stays byte-identical to upstream's,
so nothing a detector can reach moves. The new events carry a status octet and
a version octet and no credential: the username and password are not put in a
NetLog parameter here for the same reason patch 0001 kept them off
ProxyServer.

The datagram relay in patch 0079 is left alone and is not a half-instrumented
path: it emits no SOCKS events of any kind by its own design, returning the
underlying UDP socket's NetLog source so a capture gains no field that could
hold a relay address, and its authentication failures surface as the connect
result. These types are available to it if that ever changes.

Tests: the existing authenticated-handshake test now asserts that an
authentication phase appears in the log, and the existing rejected-credential
test asserts that the rejection reports as SOCKS5_AUTH_REJECTED and not as
SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH, which is the distinction the old code lost. Both are
assertions about what a net-export reader sees. Compile-unverified: this
cannot be built in this session.

Primary source at Chromium 79460ebecaa5625e57a5fb679a735659e73dc687:
- net/log/net_log_event_type_list.h: the SOCKS and SOCKS5ClientSocket blocks.
- net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc: SOCKS5ClientSocket::DoLoop and
  SOCKS5ClientSocket::DoAuthReadComplete.

Ledger: proxy.socks5-authentication (diagnostics only; no observable changes).
diff --git a/net/log/net_log_event_type_list.h b/net/log/net_log_event_type_list.h
--- a/net/log/net_log_event_type_list.h
+++ b/net/log/net_log_event_type_list.h
@@ -3357,7 +3357,35 @@
 
 // The time spent waiting for the "greeting" response from the SOCKS server.
 EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_GREET_READ)
+
+// The time spent sending the RFC 1929 username/password request to the SOCKS
+// server. Present only when the proxy selected method 0x02.
+EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_AUTH_WRITE)
+
+// The time spent waiting for the RFC 1929 authentication response.
+EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_AUTH_READ)
+
+// This event indicates that the SOCKS server closed the connection during the
+// RFC 1929 authentication exchange, after selecting that method.
+EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTEDLY_CLOSED)
+
+// This event indicates that the RFC 1929 subnegotiation version in the
+// server's authentication response was not 0x01. This is the subnegotiation's
+// own version and is unrelated to the SOCKS version. The extra parameters show
+// its value:
+//   {
+//     "version": <Integer subnegotiation version in the response>,
+//   }
+EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTED_VERSION)
 
+// This event indicates that the SOCKS server rejected the credentials it was
+// sent. The extra parameters show the RFC 1929 status octet, where any value
+// other than 0x00 is a failure:
+//   {
+//     "status": <Integer status octet in the response>,
+//   }
+EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_AUTH_REJECTED)
+
 // The time spent sending the CONNECT request to the SOCKS server.
 EVENT_TYPE(SOCKS5_HANDSHAKE_WRITE)
 
diff --git a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc
--- a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc
+++ b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc
@@ -252,17 +252,23 @@
         break;
       case STATE_AUTH_WRITE:
         DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
+        net_log_.BeginEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_WRITE);
         rv = DoAuthWrite();
         break;
       case STATE_AUTH_WRITE_COMPLETE:
         rv = DoAuthWriteComplete(rv);
+        net_log_.EndEventWithNetErrorCode(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_WRITE,
+                                          rv);
         break;
       case STATE_AUTH_READ:
         DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
+        net_log_.BeginEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_READ);
         rv = DoAuthRead();
         break;
       case STATE_AUTH_READ_COMPLETE:
         rv = DoAuthReadComplete(rv);
+        net_log_.EndEventWithNetErrorCode(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_READ,
+                                          rv);
         break;
       case STATE_HANDSHAKE_WRITE:
         DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
@@ -458,8 +464,7 @@
     return result;
 
   if (result == 0) {
-    net_log_.AddEvent(
-        NetLogEventType::SOCKS_UNEXPECTEDLY_CLOSED_DURING_GREETING);
+    net_log_.AddEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTEDLY_CLOSED);
     return ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED;
   }
 
@@ -475,15 +480,15 @@
   read_buf_.reset();
 
   // The subnegotiation carries its own version number, which RFC 1929 fixes at
-  // 0x01. It is not the SOCKS version.
+  // 0x01. It is not the SOCKS version, so it does not report as one.
   if (version != kAuthSubnegotiationVersion) {
-    net_log_.AddEventWithIntParams(NetLogEventType::SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_VERSION,
-                                   "version", version);
+    net_log_.AddEventWithIntParams(
+        NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_UNEXPECTED_VERSION, "version", version);
     return ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED;
   }
 
   if (status != kAuthStatusSuccess) {
-    net_log_.AddEventWithIntParams(NetLogEventType::SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH,
+    net_log_.AddEventWithIntParams(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_REJECTED,
                                    "status", status);
     return ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED;
   }
diff --git a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket_unittest.cc b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket_unittest.cc
--- a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket_unittest.cc
+++ b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket_unittest.cc
@@ -183,6 +183,15 @@
   rv = callback_.WaitForResult();
   EXPECT_THAT(rv, IsOk());
   EXPECT_TRUE(user_sock_->IsConnected());
+
+  // Someone reading a net-export dump has to be able to see that an
+  // authentication exchange happened at all; the greeting events alone do not
+  // say whether the proxy selected method 0x02.
+  auto net_log_entries = net_log_observer_.GetEntries();
+  EXPECT_TRUE(LogContainsEntryWithTypeAfter(
+      net_log_entries, 0, NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_WRITE));
+  EXPECT_TRUE(LogContainsEntryWithTypeAfter(
+      net_log_entries, 0, NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_READ));
 }
 
 // A proxy that rejects the credentials must fail the connection rather than
@@ -201,6 +210,15 @@
   rv = callback_.WaitForResult();
   EXPECT_THAT(rv, IsError(ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED));
   EXPECT_FALSE(user_sock_->IsConnected());
+
+  // Wrong credentials and an unsupported authentication method are different
+  // failures and a proxy diagnosis depends on telling them apart, so the
+  // rejection must not report as SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH.
+  auto net_log_entries = net_log_observer_.GetEntries();
+  EXPECT_TRUE(LogContainsEntryWithTypeAfter(
+      net_log_entries, 0, NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_AUTH_REJECTED));
+  EXPECT_FALSE(LogContainsEntryWithTypeAfter(
+      net_log_entries, 0, NetLogEventType::SOCKS_UNEXPECTED_AUTH));
 }
 
